 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Chaos erupts in two places at once: at the police station, Roma's mother spots her little boy with Max, while back at the restaurant Senya and Fedya trap German inside the walk-in fridge.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Вот же этот мальчик!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Подождите, я сейчас всё объясню.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "chef",
   "t": "Заходите, гости дорогие.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "chef",
   "t": "Мы попытались, у нас не получилось.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "german",
   "t": "Сомнительный способ меня остановить.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "german",
   "t": "Всё, я пошёл отсюда.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "chef",
   "t": "Закрывай камеру скорее!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Давайте не будем кричать.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Успокойтесь все, пожалуйста.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Это ребёнок Константина Анисимова.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Вы знаете Константина?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Тогда где наш ребёнок?",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Let's go through each line of the double predicament.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Вот же этот мальчик!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Here is this little boy!' Adding the emphatic particle right after 'here is' expresses sudden recognition.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Подождите, я сейчас всё объясню.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Wait, I will explain everything right now.' The polite plural imperative asks everyone to pause before jumping to conclusions.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "chef",
   "t": "Заходите, гости дорогие.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Come on in, dear guests.' Putting the adjective 'dear' after the noun 'guests' gives a traditional, ironic Russian welcome.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "chef",
   "t": "Мы попытались, у нас не получилось.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We tried, it did not work out for us.' Success or failure in Russian is expressed impersonally: literally 'by us it did not turn out'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "german",
   "t": "Сомнительный способ меня остановить.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'A dubious way to stop me.' The adjective 'dubious' modifies 'way/method', followed by the infinitive phrase.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "german",
   "t": "Всё, я пошёл отсюда.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'That is it, I am leaving.' In spoken Russian, saying 'I went' in the past tense means 'I'm off' or 'I'm leaving right now'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "chef",
   "t": "Закрывай камеру скорее!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Close the walk-in fridge quickly!' In a restaurant kitchen, the walk-in cooler is simply called 'the chamber'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Давайте не будем кричать.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Let's not shout.' Negative 'let's not' in Russian always uses 'let's not be' plus the imperfective infinitive.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Успокойтесь все, пожалуйста.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Calm down, everyone, please.' The reflexive command urges the whole room to settle down.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Это ребёнок Константина Анисимова.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'This is Konstantin Anisimov's child.' Both the first name and surname of the father take genitive endings to mark possession.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Вы знаете Константина?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Do you know Konstantin?' Because a person is animate, the masculine accusative direct object takes the genitive ending '-a'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Тогда где наш ребёнок?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Then where is our child?' The adverb 'then' links the realization to the urgent question 'where'. Now let's hear the whole scene again at natural speed.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
